Yes. At this point the right move is to **stop inventing agent-evolution machinery** and turn Moltwork into a very thin integration/science layer around things that already exist.

The current `ethonline-2026` branch has already crossed the architectural threshold: Campaign/SuccessModel, Git primitives, Harbor sketch, Letta runtime, molting, Hydra schema/projectors, and the frozen decisions are all there.  What it needs now is a production simplification pass.

## The production stack I would actually run

```text
                    ORACLE
          "what live work exists?"
                    │
                    ▼
                 MOLTWORK
       Campaign + budget + experiment policy
                    │
         ┌──────────┴───────────┐
         ▼                      ▼
       LETTA                  HARBOR
 persistent worker       world + execution
         │                      │
         │                Reward Kit
         │                      │
         └──────────┬───────────┘
                    ▼
               WorkerRun
                    │
         ┌──────────┼──────────────┐
         ▼          ▼              ▼
      Git state   ATIF/results   WorkerKit
                                receipt
         │          │              │
         └──────────┼──────────────┘
                    ▼
                 HYDRADB
           derived evidence graph
                    │
                    ▼
                   CGE
        "what should we try next?"
                    │
                    ▼
              candidate Git fork
```

And in v0:

**no OpenEvolve orchestration, no Agent Lightning, no A-MEM, no custom memory algorithms, no custom benchmark runtime.**

They are available later as treatments.

